## Summary

`dsp` (shorthand for `claude --dangerously-skip-permissions`) is a common workflow shortcut but is not shipped as a bin entry in the claude-flow package. Users must manually create shell-specific wrapper scripts, which is error-prone across different terminals (CMD, PowerShell, Git Bash).

## Current State

`package.json` only exports one binary:
```json
"bin": {
  "claude-flow": "./v3/@claude-flow/cli/bin/cli.js"
}
```

No `dsp` command exists anywhere in the source.

## Problem

On Windows, users need three separate scripts to make `dsp` work across all terminals:
- `.cmd` for CMD
- `.ps1` for PowerShell (VS Code default)
- extensionless bash script for Git Bash

Without these, `dsp` silently fails or returns "command not found."

## Proposed Solution

Add `dsp` as a cross-platform Node.js bin entry:

```json
"bin": {
  "claude-flow": "./v3/@claude-flow/cli/bin/cli.js",
  "dsp": "./v3/@claude-flow/cli/bin/dsp.js"
}
```

Where `dsp.js`:
```js
#!/usr/bin/env node
const { execFileSync } = require('child_process');
try {
  execFileSync('claude', ['--dangerously-skip-permissions', ...process.argv.slice(2)], { stdio: 'inherit' });
} catch (e) {
  process.exit(e.status || 1);
}
```

npm automatically generates `.cmd` and `.ps1` wrappers for Node.js bin entries on Windows, so this single file works everywhere.

## Environment

- claude-flow v3.1.0-alpha.3
- Claude Code v2.1.61
- Windows 11, PowerShell (VS Code terminal)

## Problem

`hook-handler.cjs`'s `readStdin()` uses `process.stdin.pause()` on timeout, which does NOT release the file descriptor. Node keeps a ref count on the paused stream, so when Claude Code chains multiple hooks sequentially (SessionStart runs session-restore → auto-memory-hook → etc.), the first hook's process never exits, blocking all subsequent hooks indefinitely.

This makes `auto-memory-hook.mjs import` appear to hang, but the actual culprit is `hook-handler.cjs session-restore` not exiting.

## Root Cause

```javascript
// hook-handler.cjs line 59-63 (current code)
const timer = setTimeout(() => {
  process.stdin.removeAllListeners();
  process.stdin.pause();   // ← BUG: pause() keeps fd open, Node won't exit
  resolve(data);
}, 500);
```

`process.stdin.pause()` only stops reading, but the stream reference keeps the event loop alive. When Claude Code runs hooks as `node hook-handler.cjs session-restore && node auto-memory-hook.mjs import`, the first process never terminates.

## Fix

Replace `pause()` with `destroy()` in all three paths (timeout, end, error):

```javascript
const timer = setTimeout(() => {
  process.stdin.removeAllListeners();
  try { process.stdin.destroy(); } catch (_) {}  // ← fully releases fd
  resolve(data);
}, 500);
process.stdin.on('end', () => {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  try { process.stdin.destroy(); } catch (_) {}
  resolve(data);
});
process.stdin.on('error', () => {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  try { process.stdin.destroy(); } catch (_) {}
  resolve(data);
});
```

Also add `process.stdin.destroy()` at the top of `auto-memory-hook.mjs` since it doesn't read stdin at all:

```javascript
try { process.stdin.destroy(); } catch (_) {}
```

## Measured Impact

| Before fix | After fix |
|-----------|-----------|
| SessionStart hangs indefinitely | **668ms total** (all 3 hooks complete) |
| auto-memory-hook appears to take 10-30s+ | auto-memory-hook runs in **~70ms** |
| Required `/dev/null` workaround | Works with normal pipe inheritance |

## Reproduction

```bash
# This hangs (without the fix):
node .claude/helpers/hook-handler.cjs session-restore && node .claude/helpers/auto-memory-hook.mjs import

# This works (stdin closed explicitly):
node .claude/helpers/hook-handler.cjs session-restore < /dev/null && node .claude/helpers/auto-memory-hook.mjs import
```

## Affected Files (upstream)

- `.claude/helpers/hook-handler.cjs` — `readStdin()` function
- `.claude/helpers/auto-memory-hook.mjs` — should destroy stdin on entry

## Note on @claude-flow/memory resolution

The separate issue #1306 (loadMemoryPackage can't find the package) is also real, but once the package IS installed (npm install --save-dev @claude-flow/memory), the import completes in ~70ms. The hang was masking this — users thought the package load was slow when it was actually the stdin deadlock.

