## Bug

All MCP tool files in `v3/@claude-flow/cli/src/mcp-tools/` use `process.cwd()` to build storage paths (e.g. `.claude-flow/swarm/`, `.claude-flow/agents/`). On Windows, when an AI coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini Code Assist, etc.) launches the MCP server via `npx`, `process.cwd()` resolves to `C:\Windows\System32` — a read-only system directory — causing every tool that writes state to fail with:

```
EPERM: operation not permitted, mkdir 'C:\Windows\System32\.claude-flow\swarm'
```

## Affected files (16+)

Every `get*Dir()` / storage path function across:

- `swarm-tools.ts` — `join(process.cwd(), '.claude-flow/swarm')`
- `agent-tools.ts` — `join(process.cwd(), STORAGE_DIR, AGENT_DIR)`
- `coordination-tools.ts`, `config-tools.ts`, `daa-tools.ts`, `github-tools.ts`
- `hive-mind-tools.ts`, `neural-tools.ts`, `performance-tools.ts`
- `session-tools.ts`, `system-tools.ts`, `workflow-tools.ts`
- `terminal-tools.ts`, `task-tools.ts`, `guidance-tools.ts`
- `hooks-tools.ts` (passes `process.cwd()` to `startDaemon`)

## Root cause

MCP servers are spawned as child processes by the host agent. On Windows, child processes inherit `C:\Windows\System32` as their working directory unless the host explicitly sets `cwd`. Most AI coding agents do not set `cwd` when launching MCP servers, making `process.cwd()` unreliable as a project root.

## Proposed fix

Create a shared `getProjectDir()` resolver in `mcp-tools/resolve-project-dir.ts` that all tool files import instead of calling `process.cwd()` directly. The resolver should be **agent-agnostic** — not tied to any specific AI platform:

```typescript
import { homedir } from 'node:os';

const SYSTEM_DIR_PREFIXES = ['c:\windows', 'c:/windows'];

function isSystemDir(dir: string): boolean {
  return SYSTEM_DIR_PREFIXES.some(p => dir.toLowerCase().startsWith(p));
}

let _cached: string | undefined;

export function getProjectDir(): string {
  if (_cached !== undefined) return _cached;

  // 1. Explicit project dir (agent-agnostic env var)
  //    CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR (Claude Code), CODEX_PROJECT_DIR, etc.
  for (const key of ['CLAUDE_FLOW_PROJECT_DIR', 'CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR', 'INIT_CWD']) {
    const val = process.env[key];
    if (val && !isSystemDir(val)) {
      _cached = val;
      return _cached;
    }
  }

  // 2. process.cwd() — works on macOS/Linux and most non-MCP contexts
  const cwd = process.cwd();
  if (!isSystemDir(cwd)) {
    _cached = cwd;
    return _cached;
  }

  // 3. Fall back to user home
  _cached = homedir();
  return _cached;
}
```

Then replace every `join(process.cwd(), STORAGE_DIR, ...)` with `join(getProjectDir(), STORAGE_DIR, ...)`.

**Important**: `process.cwd()` should still be used where the actual runtime working directory matters (e.g. child process `cwd` in `terminal-tools.ts`, status reporting in `system-tools.ts`). Only storage-path resolution should use the new resolver.

