## Summary

`agentdb_causal-edge` corrupts `.swarm/memory.db` — `PRAGMA integrity_check` reports **`database disk image is malformed (11)` / `2nd reference to page …`** — after a normal `memory_store` + `causal-edge` sequence through the MCP server. It reproduces on a **pristine clean install** of `@claude-flow/cli@3.12.4` (latest).

This is a **regression of #1257 / #1614** (the dual-engine / snapshot-overwrite write class). ADR-068 (#1257, 2026-03-02) moved memory onto the single native better-sqlite3 bridge to *eliminate* dual-engine writes. **ADR-130 ("Phase 1", ≈2026-05-25) reintroduced a standalone sql.js writer** for `graph_edges` that writes the same `memory.db` via whole-file `fs.writeFileSync`, while the bridge holds a better-sqlite3 WAL. The whole-file flush overwrites the main file out from under the WAL → B-tree corruption.

## Environment

- `@claude-flow/cli` **3.12.4** (npm `latest`)
- macOS arm64; reproduced on **Node 20.18.1, 22.21.1, and 24.14.1**
- `agentdb` 3.0.0-alpha.16, `@claude-flow/memory` 3.0.0-alpha.20

## Root cause

Two engines write the same `.swarm/memory.db`:

- `memory_store` → `memory-bridge.js` → `ControllerRegistry` → **AgentDB / better-sqlite3 (WAL)**
- `agentdb_causal-edge` → `mcp-tools/agentdb-tools.js: writeGraphEdge()` → `memory/graph-edge-writer.js: insertGraphEdge()` → **sql.js**:
  ```js
  // graph-edge-writer.js
  const SQL = await initSqlJs();
  db = new SQL.Database(fs.readFileSync(dbPath));   // reads MAIN only (ignores the WAL)
  ...
  fs.writeFileSync(_dbPath, Buffer.from(db.export())); // whole-file overwrite, after EVERY edge
  ```

`graph-edge-writer.js`'s own header still says *"opens the shared sql.js SQLite db (the same file used by memory-initializer storeEntry)"* — but `storeEntry` was moved to the better-sqlite3 bridge by ADR-068, so that assumption is stale. The sql.js whole-file write and the better-sqlite3 WAL now race on one file, exactly as in #1257.

The corruption lands in `graph_edges` / its indexes first (`idx_graph_edges_reinforced`, `_relation`, `_target`) and propagates to `memory_entries`, after which `memory_search` / `memory_stats` (which read through the bridge) return empty for newly-written namespaces even though the rows are physically present — the "writes succeed / reads empty" symptom.

## Steps to reproduce

```bash
mkdir /tmp/cf && cd /tmp/cf && npm init -y && npm install @claude-flow/cli@latest
node driver.cjs /tmp/cf          # 8x memory_store, then 15x agentdb_causal-edge (script below)
sqlite3 .swarm/memory.db 'PRAGMA integrity_check;'
#   Error: stepping, database disk image is malformed (11)
#   *** in database main ***  2nd reference to page 43 ...
```

`memory_store` ×8 **alone** → `integrity_check: ok`. Adding the 15 `agentdb_causal-edge` calls → **malformed**, every run.

## Ruled out (so triage doesn't chase these)

| Variable | Tested | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Node version | 20, 22, 24 (native binary loads in all) | **all corrupt** |
| `better-sqlite3` | 11.10.0 / SQLite 3.49.2 **and** 12.11.1 / SQLite 3.53.2 (via `overrides: {">=12.8.0"}`) | **both corrupt** |
| install | clean `/tmp` **and** existing | **both corrupt** |

So this is **independent of Node and of the missing-Node-24-prebuilt issue (#2219)** — purely the sql.js + better-sqlite3 dual-write reintroduced by ADR-130.

## Suggested fix

Route `graph_edges` writes through the same better-sqlite3 bridge / `ControllerRegistry` as `storeEntry` (finish the ADR-068 unification for the ADR-130 path), and retire the standalone sql.js `graph-edge-writer.js`. Same remedy that closed #1257.

