## Problem

`getPkgVersion()` in the generated statusline (`statusline.cjs`) probes, among others:

- `CWD/node_modules/ruflo/package.json`
- `CWD/node_modules/@claude-flow/cli/package.json`
- the marketplace path and global-install locations (#2221)

When Claude Code runs inside a **git worktree** (a very common multi-agent pattern — each agent gets its own worktree of the main repo), the worktree has **no `node_modules` of its own**, so both CWD probes miss. On machines without a global install or marketplace checkout, *every* candidate misses and the statusline silently shows the **baked-in fallback version** (whatever version generated the template, e.g. `3.28.0`) instead of the actually-installed one.

The drift compounds: the user upgrades the local install (`npm i ruflo@latest`) but the baked value stays frozen until the next `init` regenerates the script — so worktree sessions display an increasingly stale version while main-repo sessions show the right one.

## Repro

```bash
cd /path/to/repo            # has node_modules/ruflo at, say, 3.32.8
git worktree add /tmp/wt
cd /tmp/wt                  # no node_modules here
# render the statusline → shows the baked fallback, not 3.32.8
```

## Proposed fix (no process spawn needed)

The template's doc comments are explicit about avoiding spawns in the render path, and this doesn't need one: in a worktree, `CWD/.git` is a plain **file** containing

```
gitdir: /path/to/main/.git/worktrees/<name>
```

So the main repo root is recoverable with pure `fs`:

```js
// Worktree probe: resolve the main repo's node_modules when CWD is a linked worktree.
try {
  // walk up from CWD to find the nearest .git entry
  let dir = CWD;
  while (dir !== path.dirname(dir)) {
    const dotGit = path.join(dir, '.git');
    if (fs.existsSync(dotGit)) {
      if (fs.statSync(dotGit).isFile()) {
        const m = fs.readFileSync(dotGit, 'utf-8').match(/^gitdir:\s*(.+)$/m);
        // gitdir points at <main>/.git/worktrees/<name> → main root is 3 levels up
        const wtGitDir = m && m[1].trim();
        const idx = wtGitDir ? wtGitDir.lastIndexOf(`${path.sep}.git${path.sep}worktrees${path.sep}`) : -1;
        if (idx > 0) {
          const mainRoot = wtGitDir.slice(0, idx);
          pkgPaths.push(
            path.join(mainRoot, 'node_modules', 'ruflo', 'package.json'),
            path.join(mainRoot, 'node_modules', '@claude-flow', 'cli', 'package.json'),
          );
        }
      }
      break;
    }
    dir = path.dirname(dir);
  }
} catch { /* ignore */ }
```

This slots into the existing candidate list, so the highest-version-wins selection from the #2221 fix applies unchanged. (Relative `gitdir:` paths — rare, but possible — can be resolved against the worktree root; and `.git/worktrees/<name>/commondir` is an alternative source if you prefer.)

## Context

Same function/lineage as #2195 and #2221 — this is the remaining resolution gap. We previously carried this as a local patch (via `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`, which works but spawns); the fs-only variant above avoids the spawn entirely.

## Summary

On Windows, all MCP browser-session tools that shell out to `npx` fail immediately with `ENOENT` → surfaced as `"command not found: npx"`, even when `npx` is correctly installed and resolvable from PATH. Root cause is in `dist/src/mcp-tools/browser-session-tools.js`: its `shell()` helper calls `child_process.execFile('npx', args, opts)` with no `shell: true`. On Windows, `npx` is `npx.cmd`, and Windows cannot execute `.cmd`/`.bat` files via `execFile`/`spawn` without going through `cmd.exe` — this fails regardless of PATH contents.

This is **not a PATH/environment misconfiguration**. It reproduces on a machine where `npx --version` works fine from a normal shell, with Node's own install directory correctly on PATH.

## Environment

- OS: Windows 11 Pro (10.0.26200)
- Node: v24.18.0
- npm: 11.16.0
- Package: `@claude-flow/cli` (also shipped as `claude-flow`, `ruflo`), resolved via `npx -y @claude-flow/cli@latest` per the plugin's `.mcp.json`
- Affected file: `dist/src/mcp-tools/browser-session-tools.js`

## Reproduction

```js
import { execFile } from 'node:child_process';
import { promisify } from 'node:util';
const run = promisify(execFile);

// Exactly what shell() in browser-session-tools.js does:
await run('npx', ['--version'], { timeout: 10000, encoding: 'utf-8' });
// → Error: ENOENT, err.code === 'ENOENT'
// shell() maps this to: "command not found: npx"
```

Calling the MCP tool directly:

```json
// browser_session_record({ url: "https://example.com", task: "..." })
{
  "success": false,
  "error": "browser open failed",
  "detail": "command not found: npx",
  "stderr": ""
}
```

## Why `shell: true` is not a safe fix

The obvious quick fix — add `shell: true` to the `execFile` call — reproduces successfully:

```js
await run('npx', ['--version'], { timeout: 10000, encoding: 'utf-8', shell: true });
// → succeeds
```

But this is unsafe for this codebase specifically: with `shell: true`, Node does **not** escape array args — it space-joins them into a raw command line. One of the five call sites passes an **unescaped, user-supplied URL** straight through:

```js
// browser_session_record handler
const npxBo = await shell('npx', ['--yes', 'agent-browser', '--session', sessionId, '--json', 'open', input.url], { timeout: 60000 });
```

`input.url` is caller-controlled. With `shell: true`, a crafted URL containing shell metacharacters (`&`, `|`, backticks, `$()`, etc.) becomes a command-injection vector on the host machine — the same class of issue as Node's own advisory for `child_process` + `.bat`/`.cmd` on Windows (GHSA-hp2h-p3jx-hh8j / CVE-2024-27980). `shell: true` should not be applied here without also hand-rolling correct `cmd.exe` argument quoting, which is error-prone and easy to get subtly wrong.

## Root cause detail

There are 5 call sites in the file that shell out via the bare `'npx'` command:

1. `ruvector()` fallback when no local `ruvector` install is found
2. `browser_session_record` → `npx --yes agent-browser --session … --json open <url>` (the URL-injection-risk one)
3. `browser_session_end` → `npx -y @claude-flow/cli@latest memory store …`
4. `browser_template_apply` → `npx -y @claude-flow/cli@latest memory retrieve --namespace browser-templates …`
5. `browser_cookie_use` → `npx -y @claude-flow/cli@latest memory retrieve --namespace browser-cookies …`

All five hit the same Windows ENOENT.

Note the file already has a working precedent for avoiding this class of bug: `resolveLocalRuvectorCli()` resolves a locally-installed `ruvector` package's bin and invokes it via `spawn(process.execPath, [cli, ...args])` — i.e. `node <script>`, a real executable, no shell involved. The same pattern generalizes cleanly to `npx` itself.

## Proposed fix

Resolve Node's own bundled `npx-cli.js` (ships alongside `node.exe` in every Node install: `<node.exe dir>/node_modules/npm/bin/npx-cli.js`) and invoke it directly via `node <npx-cli.js> <args>` — bypassing the `.cmd` shim and any shell entirely, so args stay literal argv (no injection surface) and no shell resolution is needed on any platform.

```js
// Resolve node's own npx-cli.js (ships with every Node install next to node.exe).
// Invoking it via `node <js>` is a real executable — no shell, no PATHEXT/.cmd
// resolution needed, and args stay literal argv (no injection surface from
// unescaped user input, unlike `execFile('npx', ..., {shell:true})`).
let npxCliJsPromise = null;
function resolveNpxCliJs() {
    if (npxCliJsPromise) return npxCliJsPromise;
    npxCliJsPromise = (async () => {
        try {
            const path = await import('node:path');
            const { existsSync } = await import('node:fs');
            const candidate = path.join(path.dirname(process.execPath), 'node_modules', 'npm', 'bin', 'npx-cli.js');
            return existsSync(candidate) ? candidate : null;
        } catch {
            return null;
        }
    })();
    return npxCliJsPromise;
}

async function npxShell(args, opts = {}) {
    const cliJs = await resolveNpxCliJs();
    if (cliJs) return shell(process.execPath, [cliJs, ...args], opts);
    return shell('npx', args, opts); // fallback — original behavior
}
```

Then replace all 5 `shell('npx', [...], opts)` call sites with `npxShell([...], opts)`.

## Verification

Reproduced and verified locally:

```js
// FAILS — exact original bug:
await run('npx', ['--version'], { timeout: 10000, encoding: 'utf-8' });
// → ENOENT

// Also FAILS — execFile still can't launch .cmd directly without a shell:
await run('npx.cmd', ['--version'], { timeout: 10000, encoding: 'utf-8' });
// → EINVAL

// SUCCEEDS — the proposed fix:
const npxCliJs = path.join(path.dirname(process.execPath), 'node_modules', 'npm', 'bin', 'npx-cli.js');
await run(process.execPath, [npxCliJs, '--version'], { timeout: 10000, encoding: 'utf-8' });
// → "11.16.0"
```

After patching all 5 call sites and reconnecting the MCP server, `browser_session_record` opens URLs successfully with no error.

## Additional notes

- `agent-browser` (the package `browser_session_record` shells out to via `npx --yes agent-browser`) was not installed anywhere locally in my test environment, meaning every invocation cold-pulls an unvetted package from the public npm registry on first use. Separate concern from this bug, but worth flagging since it's on the same code path.
- Because the MCP server is launched via `npx -y @claude-flow/cli@latest` (per the plugin's `.mcp.json`), npx's local package cache (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\npm-cache\_npx\<hash>`) can resolve to a *different* content-hash directory across reconnects/publishes of `@latest` — so any local/manual patch to the cache is inherently non-durable. The real fix needs to land in the published source and propagate through all three published packages (`@claude-flow/cli`, `claude-flow`, `ruflo`) per this repo's own publishing rules.
- Worth a quick audit of `browser-intent-tools.js` (imports from the same module) for the same `shell('npx', …)` pattern, in case it shells out similarly — I didn't check it directly.

